---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Delmain case study — Evelyn Rodríguez">

  <article class="case-study">
    <a href="/#work" class="case-back">← Back to work</a>

    <p class="eyebrow">Case study — Delmain</p>
    <h1>70+ production WordPress sites, kept correct and stable</h1>
    <p class="case-meta">Web Developer · Nov 2024 – Nov 2025 · WordPress · HTML/CSS/JS · Workflow automation</p>
    <a href="https://jacksonfamilydentalonline.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="case-live">Visit the live site <span class="arrow arrow-out">↗</span></a>

    <img src="/delmain1.png" alt="Jackson Family Dental website homepage" class="case-img" loading="lazy" />

    <div class="case-body">
      <h2>Context</h2>
      <p>
        Delmain is a digital marketing agency in Portland, Oregon, working exclusively with dental practices across the US, more than 200 of them. Their client sites are production businesses: when a form breaks or a page renders wrong, the practice loses patients. I joined as a web developer in November 2024 and stayed through November 2025.
      </p>

      <h2>My role</h2>
      <p>
        I was responsible for maintaining and extending more than 70 live WordPress sites. That meant working across whatever each site was built with (Divi, Elementor, WPBakery, Avada, Gutenberg, and plenty of custom HTML, CSS, and JavaScript) without breaking what already worked. Typical week: responsive fixes, layout bugs, new sections and landing pages, plugin and theme updates, and content changes that had to go live without downtime. I didn't build these sites from scratch; I owned keeping them correct, current, and stable.
      </p>

      <h2>One story</h2>
      <p>
        Publishing changes across that many sites was slow and manual: edit, screenshot, QA checklist, email the account manager, repeat. Forms were the riskiest part. A silent SMTP failure meant a client's leads vanished without anyone noticing. I automated the repetitive parts of the publishing and QA workflow and standardized SMTP integrations across sites, so form delivery was verified instead of assumed. What had taken a full afternoon of manual checking became a routine that ran the same way every time.
      </p>

      <h2>Outcome</h2>
      <p>
        Fewer regressions reaching clients, form failures caught before clients noticed, and hours of manual QA per week returned to actual development work. And a habit I kept: treat maintenance as engineering, not chores.
      </p>
    </div>

    <a href="/#contact" class="case-cta">Work with me →</a>
  </article>

</MainLayout>
